  "minutes": 22,
  "title": {
    "en": "Eastern vagrants"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Siberian Accentor, Red-flanked Bluetail, two pipits, two buntings, Red-breasted Flycatcher and Brown Shrike"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Easterly winds in October bring birds from Siberia to the coasts of north-west Europe. These birds were migrating south-east to the tropics of Asia, and some pointed the wrong way: 'reversed migration'. In the autumn of 2016 thousands of Siberian Accentors arrived in western Europe, and the event is still talked about.\n\nThe birds of today are the ones that you may well find: Richard's and Olive-backed Pipit, Little and Rustic Bunting, Red-breasted Flycatcher. The bluetail and the accentor are rare, and the Brown Shrike is a mega. On the day, when the wind has gone round to the east, check hedges and bushes on the east coast of the UK and the Dutch, German and Danish coasts, the Swedish islands and Norway.\n\nLook for oddities: a bird that does not fit what you know. The first thing to do is to take a photograph, then look at the head, the tail and the call."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "eye ring"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A pale ring round the eye. Bold in Little Bunting."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "breast band"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A band across the breast, as in the chestnut band of a Rustic Bunting."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "mask"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A dark patch through the eye, like a bandit's mask. Brown Shrikes have a strong one."
      }
    }
  ],
